@node Glossary
@chapter Glossary
@cindex glossary

@table @dfn

@item Agent
A software system that perceives inputs, plans actions, and executes tools
to accomplish a goal.  Codebud's agent receives natural-language requests
and acts on the local filesystem.

@item AgentCore
The central Python class in @file{src/codebud/core.py} that owns all subsystems
(planner, safety engine, executor, memory, tool registry, sandbox) and
exposes @code{handle_user_message()} to the CLI.

@item BM25
A classic keyword-ranking formula: documents score higher the more often
they contain the query's words, and rare words count more than common
ones.  Codebud's project index uses SQLite's FTS5 implementation of it for
exact identifiers and error messages, where embeddings are weak.

@item Chunk
Two meanings.  (1) A piece of a file in the project index: a Python
function or class, a manual node, a Markdown section, or a window of
lines, stored with its path and line range (@pxref{The index
Subcommand}).  (2) A fragment of text returned by the model during
streaming generation; Codebud's @code{on_chunk} callback receives each
one as it arrives and forwards it to the @code{ProgressIndicator}.

@item Code fence
Markdown syntax that wraps a code block: @code{```json ... ```}.  Some
models wrap their JSON output in code fences despite being instructed not
to.  @code{LLMPlanner._strip_code_fence()} removes them before parsing.

@item Context window
The maximum number of tokens an LLM can process in a single call --- both
input (prompt) and output (generated text) combined.  The 3B Qwen model
has a context window of approximately 32,768 tokens.  Ollama loads models
with a smaller one (@code{num_ctx}) unless asked; Codebud asks for 8,192 on
every request (@env{CODEBUD_NUM_CTX}).

@item Cosine similarity
A measure of similarity between two vectors: the cosine of the angle
between them, ranging from @minus{}1 (opposite) to +1 (identical
direction).  Used by @code{search_embeddings} to rank stored entries
against a query embedding.

@item Doctor
The @command{codebud doctor} subcommand.  It runs a series of health checks
(including where data can leave the machine) and prints a @samp{[PASS]}, @samp{[FAIL]} or @samp{[WARN]} line for each, with
a one-line fix command for every failure or warning.  Warnings (such as a
missing embedding model) do not count as failures.

@item Editable install
A @command{pip install -e .} installation where the package's source
directory is added directly to @code{sys.path} so changes to the source
files are reflected immediately without reinstalling.  The @command{codebud}
binary is placed in @file{.venv/bin/}.

@item Embedding
A fixed-length list of floating-point numbers that encodes the semantic
meaning of a piece of text.  Texts with similar meaning have embeddings
that point in similar directions in high-dimensional space.  Codebud
generates embeddings with Ollama's @code{/api/embed} endpoint.

@item Encryption at rest
Storing chat sessions, the step history and project indexes encrypted
with an age key that
your passphrase seals (@code{encrypt = true}, off by default).  Saving
needs no passphrase; reading asks once per run.  @xref{Encryption at
Rest}.

@item Privacy level
The second dial next to the mode: @code{locked} (nothing leaves this
machine), @code{private} (the default) or @code{trusted} (no privacy
prompts).  It never lifts the safety rules.  @xref{Privacy Levels}.

@item Project context
The chunks of the project index most relevant to a request, added to the
planning prompt (and to plain chat replies) as reference material.  Off
with @env{CODEBUD_RAG}=0, @option{--no-rag} or @code{/rag off}.

@item Project index
A per-project SQLite database under @file{$XDG_DATA_HOME/codebud/index/},
built by @command{codebud index}.  It stores the project's files as
chunks (functions, classes, manual nodes, headings), each with an
embedding vector and a full-text index.  The @code{search_embeddings} tool
searches it.  @xref{The index Subcommand}.

@item Executor
The Python class in @file{src/codebud/executor.py} that walks a validated plan
and calls each tool in sequence.  It stops on the first non-zero return
code and records every completed step in the @code{MemoryStore}.

@item Few-shot example
A worked example embedded in the LLM's prompt that demonstrates the
expected input/output format.  Codebud's planner prompt contains five
examples covering common request patterns.  Few-shot examples are the most
reliable way to steer a small model toward correct JSON output.

@item Generation phase
The second phase of an LLM call.  After the model has processed the entire
input prompt (prefill), it generates output tokens one at a time.  The
@code{ProgressIndicator} shows a token counter and tokens-per-second rate
during this phase.

@item Harmless commands
Commands listed in @file{harmless_commands.json}: they only read or
report, and run without confirmation.  Arguments that are always refused
(@code{find -exec}, @dots{}) still apply.

@item Hybrid search
Search that combines several rankings.  Codebud's project search fuses
embedding similarity, BM25 keyword matches and exact symbol names with
reciprocal-rank fusion.

@item Isolation
Running each @code{command} step in a bubblewrap sandbox: no network
unless declared, read-only file system except the project, secrets
hidden.  On at privacy levels @code{private} and @code{locked}.
@xref{Isolated Steps}.

@item JSON plan
The structured output produced by the @code{LLMPlanner}: a dict with
@code{"status": "ok"} and a @code{"plan"} array of step dicts, each
containing @code{id}, @code{description}, @code{tool}, and @code{args}.

@item LLM (Large Language Model)
A neural network trained on large amounts of text that can generate
human-like text continuations given a prompt.  Codebud uses Qwen2.5-Coder
served by Ollama.

@item Memory timeline
The persistent log in @file{$XDG_DATA_HOME/codebud/memory/entries.jsonl}.  After each
executed step, the @code{MemoryStore} appends a snapshot with the
timestamp, step metadata, and tool output.  The timeline accumulates
across sessions.

@item Model
In the context of Codebud, @dfn{model} refers to a specific set of LLM
weights --- for example, @code{qwen2.5-coder:3b-instruct-q4_K_M}.  The
model is identified by name in @env{OLLAMA_MODEL} and served by the Ollama
server.

@item on_chunk
A Python callback @code{Callable[[str], None]} that the CLI passes through
the call stack (AgentCore → LLMPlanner → call_llm → _call_ollama) to
receive streaming token fragments without coupling display logic to the LLM
layer.

@item MRR (mean reciprocal rank)
A retrieval measure: for each question, 1 divided by the rank of the
first correct result (1 when it comes first, 1/2 when second, 0 when it
is missing), averaged over all questions.  @xref{Project Search
Evaluation}.

@item Ollama
A lightweight local server that loads LLM model weights into RAM and
exposes them through a JSON REST API on @code{localhost:11434}.  Codebud
uses Ollama's @code{/api/generate} endpoint (streaming) and
@code{/api/embed} endpoint (embeddings).

@item Plan error
The planner's result @code{@{"status": "plan_error", "error": "..."@}} when
it cannot produce a valid plan.  Common causes: the model returned empty
output, invalid JSON, or a malformed plan, or it declined the request
itself.  @code{AgentCore} turns it into a plain chat reply
(@code{@{"status": "chat_reply"@}}).  A plan the agent mode or the safety
policy rejects is returned as @code{plan_refused} instead, with a message
naming the step and the reason.

@item Plan invalid
The planner's result @code{@{"status": "plan_invalid", "error": "..."@}}
when steps miss arguments their tools require, even after the model was
told once what was wrong.  Reported directly, never executed.
@xref{Invalid Plan}.

@item Plan step
One element of the @code{"plan"} array in a JSON plan.  Each step
specifies one tool call: @code{id}, @code{description}, @code{tool},
and @code{args}.

@item Planner
@code{LLMPlanner} in @file{src/codebud/planner.py}.  It builds the prompt,
calls the LLM, strips code fences, parses JSON, validates the plan
structure, and retries once on parse failure.

@item Powerful commands
Commands listed in @file{powerful_commands.json}.  They are
permitted by the @code{SafetyEngine}, but because they run code or change
files they need an explicit confirmation in @code{auto} mode and in
@command{codebud run} (unless @option{--yes}).

@item Prefill phase
The first phase of an LLM call.  The model processes the entire input
prompt before generating any output.  On a CPU-only laptop this takes
about 30 s for Codebud's first planning request of a session and about 2 s
afterwards, when Ollama reuses the cached start of the prompt.  The @code{ProgressIndicator} shows a
spinner and elapsed time during this phase.

@item ProgressIndicator
The class in @file{src/codebud/cli/display.py} that runs a background thread to
display a spinner during prefill and a token counter during generation.
It writes to @code{stderr} using @code{\r} to update in place without
scrolling.

@item Prompt
The text sent to the LLM.  Codebud's planning prompt contains critical
rules, a tool catalogue, safety rules, a JSON response format, five
few-shot examples, recent conversation history, and the user's request.

@item Quantisation
A compression technique that reduces model weight precision from 32-bit
floats to 4- or 8-bit integers.  @code{q4_K_M} means 4-bit quantisation
using the K-quants method (medium variant).  Quantisation reduces the model
file size and RAM requirement by roughly 8x with modest accuracy
loss.

@item RAG (retrieval-augmented generation)
Giving a language model text retrieved for the request --- for Codebud,
project context from the index --- so that it answers from the real
source instead of guessing.

@item Recall@@k
A retrieval measure: the share of questions for which a correct result
is among the top @var{k}.  @xref{Project Search Evaluation}.

@item Reciprocal-rank fusion (RRF)
A way to merge ranked lists: each result scores
@math{1 / (60 + rank)} in every list it appears in, and the sums decide
the merged order.  Codebud counts exact symbol-name matches double and
chunks from test files half.

@item Sandbox
The @code{Sandbox} class in @file{src/codebud/sandbox.py}.  It wraps
@code{subprocess.run()}, enforces a 10-second timeout, refuses
catastrophic commands again (@code{safety.catastrophic_reason()}), and
prevents shell expansion by using @code{shlex.split()}.

@item SafetyEngine
The @code{SafetyEngine} class in @file{src/codebud/safety.py}.  It loads the
three policy JSON files and applies whole-word validation to every
@code{command} step in a plan.

@item Secret masking
Replacing API keys, tokens, private keys and passwords with
@samp{[redacted]} before chat sessions and step history are saved.  On by
default (@env{CODEBUD_MASK_SECRETS}); prompts sent to the model are not
changed.  @xref{Files and Directories}.

@item Skill
A @file{SKILL.md} file (Markdown with a @code{name}/@code{description}
frontmatter) that teaches an agent such as Claude Code or OpenClaw when and
how to use a tool.  Codebud ships a default skill (install with
@command{codebud skill install --for claude} or @code{openclaw}) and an
Agensi marketplace hand-off variant (@command{codebud skill show --for agensi};
@pxref{The skill Subcommand}).

@item Step error
The response @code{@{"status": "step_error", "error": "..."@}} returned by
@code{Executor.execute_plan()} when a tool call returns a non-zero exit
code or raises an exception.  Execution stops at the failed step.

@item Streaming
Receiving LLM output token-by-token as it is generated rather than waiting
for the complete response.  Codebud uses @code{stream=True} in the Ollama
API call so that the HTTP read timeout never fires during the long prefill
phase on CPU-only hardware.

@item Token
The fundamental unit of LLM input and output.  Roughly one word, or
one to four characters for English text.  Codebud's planning prompts are
about 1,080 tokens on the first turn of a session; generated plans are
50--100 tokens.

@item Token rate
Tokens generated per second during the generation phase.  For the 3B model
on an i7-8550U CPU the typical rate is approximately 1.1@tie{}t/s.

@item Tool
A Python class with a @code{run(args: dict) -> dict} method that performs
one operation.  Tools are the only way the agent interacts with the outside
world.  The six built-in tools are @code{file_write}, @code{file_read},
@code{command}, @code{patch}, @code{web_search}, and
@code{search_embeddings}.

@item ToolRegistry
The @code{ToolRegistry} class in @file{src/codebud/tools/tool_registry.py}.
It holds the seven tool instances and provides @code{get()}, @code{has()},
and @code{describe_tools()} to the planner and executor.

@item Unified diff
A standard text format for representing changes to a file.  Lines starting
with @code{-} were removed; lines starting with @code{+} were added.
The @code{patch} tool applies unified diffs produced by @command{diff -u}
or @command{git diff}.

@item usage_hint
A class attribute on every tool that gives the planner concrete guidance:
when to use the tool, what arguments it requires, and a one-line example.
@code{ToolRegistry.describe_tools()} includes these hints in the planner
prompt.

@end table
